"""dedup — reclaim disk by making byte-identical files share storage (APFS).

NOTHING IS EVER DELETED. Every file stays exactly where it is, fully readable,
with its own inode, permissions and mtime. Duplicates are replaced by APFS
CLONES of one donor copy, so the identical content occupies disk ONCE.

  cp/cat/ls/grep/Finder all behave identically afterwards. Writing to any copy
  is copy-on-write, so it can never affect the others (unlike hardlinks, which
  is why this tool never uses them).

  `du` will NOT shrink -- it sums each file's own block count and cannot see
  sharing. Only `df` tells the truth. This tool reports both.

Requires APFS. All paths in one run must live on the same volume.
"""

import atexit as _atexit, sys as _sys
# Trailing gap — one blank line after output, TTY only, so piped or redirected
# output stays exactly the data.
_atexit.register(lambda: _sys.stdout.isatty() and print())
